---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHero from "@/components/PageHero.astro";
import { SITE } from "@/lib/site";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Security architecture"
  description="How Fluera encrypts your canvases at rest and in transit, and how cloud sync works. sqlite3mc, TLS, EU-hosted sync, opt-in telemetry."
>

  <PageHero
    kicker="Architecture"
    title="How Fluera protects your canvases."
    description={`This page describes the security model at the level a technical reader — a security officer, a university CISO, a curious developer — would need. For the non-technical summary, start at <a href="/security">/security</a>.`}
    breadcrumb={{ label: "Security", href: "/security" }}
  />

  <article class="py-16 md:py-20 border-b border-white/[0.04]">
    <div class="container-prose prose-ink text-lg">

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-paper-50 mt-12 mb-4">Threat model</h2>
      <p>
        We assume device theft, network interception, and accidental exposure via backups or cloud sync. We assume the user's account credentials may be compromised. We do <em>not</em> assume state-level adversaries with physical access to unlocked devices — Fluera is not a secrecy tool, it is a study tool.
      </p>
      <p>
        Within that model, three guarantees:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The local database is unreadable without the key.</strong> A stolen device yields an encrypted database. Photos, PDFs and lesson audio are protected by the operating system, not by that key.</li>
        <li><strong>Synced data stays in the EU and is never sold.</strong> Cloud sync is encrypted in transit (TLS) and at rest on EU infrastructure, but it is <em>not</em> end-to-end encrypted — Fluera, as data controller, can technically access synced content. We never sell it or use it for advertising.</li>
        <li><strong>Telemetry is by consent and never carries content.</strong> It is tied to your account identifier so that you can see and delete it: it is not anonymous.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-paper-50 mt-12 mb-4">At rest: sqlite3mc, 256-bit key</h2>
      <p>
        The local SQLite database that holds your canvases — strokes, text, formulas, your study — is encrypted with SQLite3 Multiple Ciphers (sqlite3mc) in its ChaCha20-Poly1305 scheme: every page of the database is encrypted and authenticated. Photos, PDFs and lesson audio are stored as files in the app's private folder: the operating system protects them, not this key.
      </p>
      <p>
        The key is 256 random bits generated on your device and kept in the platform's secure key store — Keychain on iOS/macOS, Keystore on Android, DPAPI on Windows, libsecret on Linux. It is a raw key, not derived from your account password, and it is never sent to Fluera's servers. So that a new phone does not cost you your canvases, it also has a copy in the encrypted system backup: on Android in Block Store, saved to your Google account only when that backup is end-to-end encrypted with your screen lock; on iOS in the encrypted iCloud or Finder backup.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-paper-50 mt-12 mb-4">In transit: TLS</h2>
      <p>
        Traffic between the app and our services — sign-in, sync, the AI proxy — travels over HTTPS (TLS).
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-paper-50 mt-12 mb-4">Cross-device sync: one switch for the account</h2>
      <p>
        <em>Sync across your devices</em> is a single switch for the whole account (Settings → Data and sync), off until you turn it on, and it needs the Cloud plan. Your canvases are stored on Supabase (EU region, eu-north-1), encrypted in transit with TLS and at rest at the infrastructure level. It is <em>not</em> end-to-end encrypted: Fluera, as data controller, can technically access synced content. We never sell it or use it for advertising. Browser folds are the exception: they travel end-to-end encrypted.
      </p>
      <p>
        A canvas is written on one device at a time: on the others it opens read-only until you tap <em>Write here</em>. If the same canvas changed on two devices while they were offline, nothing is overwritten: the missing work stays as a separate canvas in the gallery, for you to merge by hand. The full guide: <a href="/docs/cloud-sync">Sync across your devices</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        A plain <code>.fluera</code> export is not encrypted: treat it like any other file. The <em>Encrypted backup</em> command writes a <code>.fluerabackup</code> file sealed with AES-256-GCM under a key kept in this device's key store; it opens only on a device that holds that key.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-paper-50 mt-12 mb-4">P2P collaboration: direct, encrypted</h2>
      <p>
        Real-time collaboration uses WebRTC DataChannels, encrypted with DTLS. Supabase Realtime acts as a signalling broker only — connection setup — not a relay for the actual canvas traffic. After the handshake, canvas edits flow peer-to-peer. The server does not see them.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is no relay server: public STUN servers (Google) only help the two devices find a route to each other. If a network blocks a direct connection, the session does not connect.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-paper-50 mt-12 mb-4">AI calls: proxied, processed in the EU</h2>
      <p>
        AI features (Socratic mode, Ghost Map, LaTeX OCR, Exam Session) use Google Gemini models served via Google Vertex AI, processed in the EU (europe-west4 NL / europe-west1 BE). Calls transit a proxy that holds credentials server-side, so client devices never see them. The proxy enforces per-plan rate limits and logs call durations for accounting — never the content of the canvas.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you opt out of AI features in <em>Settings → Privacy</em>, no canvas content is ever sent for AI processing, even for on-device OCR fallbacks.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-paper-50 mt-12 mb-4">Telemetry: by consent, allowlisted, no content</h2>
      <p>
        Product analytics are off until you consent. When on, events are limited to a server-side allowlist (session start and end, feature use, AI calls with duration and tokens — never content). They carry a random session ID and your account identifier, so that you can see and delete your own telemetry. Crash reports (Sentry, a separate consent) receive only a SHA-256 hash of that identifier. Withdrawing consent deletes the telemetry collected with your account; in any case events are kept at most 180 days.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-paper-50 mt-12 mb-4">Data recovery</h2>
      <p>
        If you change phone or reinstall and restore a system backup, the database and its key come back together and your canvases reopen as they were at the last system backup. They do not come back if the system backup is off, on Android without Google Play services or with app data over the 25 MB backup limit, if you clear the app's data, or if you uninstall without a restore. Then the local database stays unreadable — to you and to us alike — and Sync across your devices is the recovery path: sign in on the new device and your synced canvases come back. There is no separate sync passphrase to remember or lose.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-paper-50 mt-12 mb-4">Responsible disclosure</h2>
      <p>
        Security researchers are welcome. Report vulnerabilities to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.company.securityEmail}`}>{SITE.company.securityEmail}</a>. We reply within 5 working days. There is no bug bounty programme.
      </p>
      <p>
        Scope: the Fluera app (all platforms), the sync service, the AI proxy, and this marketing site. Out of scope: third-party services (Supabase, Google, Apple, Sentry, RevenueCat) — report those to the respective vendors.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-paper-50 mt-12 mb-4">External assessments</h2>
      <p>
        Fluera does not currently hold SOC 2 or ISO 27001 certification. These are on the Enterprise roadmap and will be announced publicly when complete. We prefer not to claim controls we have not independently verified.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <a href="/security/sub-processors">sub-processor list</a> is published and kept current. The Data Processing Agreement is available on request via <a href={`mailto:${SITE.company.privacyEmail}`}>{SITE.company.privacyEmail}</a>.
      </p>

    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="py-16 border-t border-white/[0.04] bg-ink-900/40">
    <div class="container-narrow text-center">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-4">
        <a href="/security/sub-processors" class="btn-secondary">Sub-processors →</a>
        <a href="/contact" class="btn-primary">Talk to us about deployment</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</BaseLayout>
